[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Performance Optimization for Large Codebases in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As TypeScript codebases grow, build times and type-checking latency can severely impact developer productivity. Two of the most powerful native tools for scaling TypeScript performance are Project References and incremental compilation."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Project References"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript Project References allow you to structure a monolithic codebase into smaller, decoupled sub-projects. This enables the TypeScript compiler (tsc) to build dependencies in the correct order and skip rebuilding unchanged projects, drastically reducing iteration times."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enables logical separation of code (e.g., separating utilities, data access layers, and UI components)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Allows parallel builds and selective type-checking."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverages composite projects to generate .d.ts.map and declaration files faster."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Project References"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use Project References, you must set "composite": true in the tsconfig.json of your sub-projects, and reference them from a root tsconfig.json file."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "composite": true,
    "declaration": true,
    "declarationMap": true,
    "strict": true
  }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In your root tsconfig.json, combine the sub-projects using the references array:"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"{
  "files": [],
  "references": [
    { "path": "./packages/utils" },
    { "path": "./packages/api" },
    { "path": "./apps/web" }
  ]
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Leveraging Incremental Compilation"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The "incremental": true flag instructs the TypeScript compiler to save information about the project graph from the last compilation to a .tsbuildinfo file. On subsequent builds, tsc only rechecks files that have changed or whose dependencies have changed."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "incremental": true,
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./.tsbuildinfo"
  }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Building and Watching via CLI"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"When working with project references, always use the -b (build) flag. The build flag tells TypeScript to inspect the project graph, build out-of-order dependencies, and apply incremental updates."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"tsc -b --watch"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining Project References with incremental compilation, you create a blazing-fast development pipeline capable of scaling gracefully across massive enterprise TypeScript applications."}]
